Name: frameDurations

Type: property

Syntax: set the frameDurations of <image> to <durationsList>

Syntax: get the frameDurations of <image>

Summary:
Specifies how long each frame of an <animated GIF> <image> stays on
screen.

Associations: image

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, android

Platforms: desktop, server, mobile

Example:
put the frameDurations of image "Walk" -- 100, 100, 100 and 200, one a line

Example:
-- every frame of a sprite at 80 milliseconds
local tDurations
repeat the frameCount of image "Demon"
   put 80 & return after tDurations
end repeat
set the frameDurations of image "Demon" to tDurations

Example:
set the frameDurations of image "Spin" to empty -- back to the GIF's own

Value:
The <frameDurations> of an <image> is a list of whole numbers of
milliseconds, one per <line>, a line for each frame of the <animated
GIF>, starting with the first. It is empty for an <image> that is not
animated.

Description:
Use the <frameDurations> <property> to read the delays an <animated GIF>
was made with, or to give its frames delays of your own.

Each frame of an animated GIF has a delay of its own, in hundredths of a
second, and an <image> shows each frame for that long. The
<frameDurations> gives those delays in milliseconds: a GIF frame with a
delay of 10 has a duration of 100.

Set the <frameDurations> to a duration for each frame. A fraction is
rounded to the nearest millisecond. An empty <line> leaves that frame
with the GIF's own delay, and so do the frames after the last line of the
list; lines past the last frame are ignored. Set the <frameDurations> to
empty to give every frame its GIF's own delay again. Setting it to
anything that is not a list of numbers of 0 or more causes an error.

The new durations take effect when the next frame comes up, while the
<image> is animating. The <playRate> of the <image> then divides each of
them: the <frameDurations> itself does not change with the <playRate>.

The <frameDurations> is not saved with the <stack>, and it is not one of
the <properties> of an <image>. Set it in a <preOpenCard> or
<openCard> <handler>, for example, to have it whenever the <stack> is
opened. A <clone> of the <image> has the same setting; an <image> pasted
from the clipboard does not, as it is made from the <image> as it would
be saved. Without
it, an <animated GIF> plays at the pace of its own delays, as it always
has.

A frame with a duration of 0 shows only until the engine next has time
for the animation, which many GIF makers write for "as fast as possible"
and web browsers show at about 100 milliseconds. To play such a GIF at a
browser's pace, set the durations of those frames to 100.

